# Authorization

Four attributes exist in `LoadlyAggregator.Infrastructure.Authorization`:

| Attribute                | Use for                                                    |
|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| `[Authorize]`            | Class-level: require an authenticated user. Always present on controllers that aren't public. |
| `[HasPermission("p")]`   | Per-action: require **this specific permission** (standard case). |
| `[HasAnyPermission(...)]`| Per-action: endpoint serves two closely-related permissions (e.g. upsert = create *or* update). |
| `[HasRole("r")]`         | Per-action: coarse role check only when permission granularity doesn't fit. Prefer `[HasPermission]`. |
| `[AllowAnonymous]`       | Public endpoints only (`AppVersion`, signed-URL downloads, auth entry points). |

The permission filter supports user types `admin`, `company`, `driver`, `collector` — the JWT `userType` claim drives routing to the right table.

## Permission naming — strict rules

- Format: `verb_entity_in_snake_case`.
- Entity is **singular**: `create_company`, `view_tag`, `delete_driver`, `edit_vehicle_insurance`, `view_contractor_payout`.
- Verb comes from this vocabulary (extend only with team review):
  - CRUD: `create_`, `view_`, `edit_`, `delete_` (prefer `edit_` over `update_`).
  - Workflow: `activate_`, `deactivate_`, `cancel_`, `terminate_`, `import_`, `send_`, `upload_`, `download_`, `manage_`, `push_`, `self_complete_`.
- One permission may be shared across multiple endpoints of the same workflow (`self_complete_trip` for all trip-lifecycle actions; `view_dashboard` for every dashboard GET).
- Define the string **once**, as `public const string Permission = "verb_entity";` on the Request class. Controllers reference `RequestClass.Permission` only.

## Usage pattern

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/admin/invoices")]
[Authorize]                                                   // class-level: require auth
public class InvoicesController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpPost]
    [HasPermission(CreateInvoiceRequest.Permission)]          // = "create_invoice"
    public async Task<ActionResult> Create([FromBody] CreateInvoiceRequest req) { ... }

    [HttpPut("{contractId:long}/draft")]
    [HasAnyPermission(                                        // true "upsert" endpoint
        UpsertDraftContractBillingSnapshotRequest.Permission,
        UpdateDraftContractBillingSnapshotRequest.Permission)]
    public async Task<ActionResult> UpsertDraft(long contractId, ...) { ... }
}
```

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ Hardcoding a string: `[HasPermission("create_invoice")]` — always via `RequestClass.Permission`.
- ❌ Plural entity: `create_admins`, `view_tags`. Use `create_admin`, `view_tag`. (Rare legacy plurals exist; don't propagate.)
- ❌ Omitting `[HasPermission]` on an authenticated action — `[Authorize]` alone grants anyone with a valid token.
- ❌ `[HasAnyPermission]` as a lazy shortcut when two unrelated endpoints happen to share handlers — split them or combine the permissions in the role config instead.
- ❌ `[HasRole("admin")]` to gate CRUD on an entity — use the matching permission instead; roles are grants of permissions, not an auth primitive.
- ❌ `[AllowAnonymous]` on anything that touches user data — reserve for truly public endpoints.
